// `&(2i64 + 3i64)` returned from a function is a borrow of a value with no
// place of its own — the operator result lives in the frame and dies with it.
//
// This ADMITTED until 2026-08-25 and dangled at runtime: with a clobbering call
// between the borrow and the read, the exit code tracked the clobber constant
// (9 -> 9, 40 -> 40, 71 -> 71) where 5 is correct.
//
// The omission was BY SPELLING. `is_temporary_value_expr` listed LitInt, the
// aggregate literals and the call forms, and stopped — so a BinOp fell past it
// to `value_local_root`, found no local root, and reached the tail comment
// "unknown — conservative-accept". An operator produces a fresh value exactly
// as a literal or a call does; the property was never in doubt, only the list.
//
// ⚠ REFUSED RATHER THAN PROMOTED, DELIBERATELY. Logos has NO const-eval: a
// `const` is stored as an expression and read by name, and compile-time
// arithmetic goes through `metacall` (project_no_const_eval, Victor
// 2026-05-11). Folding `2+3` inside the promotion predicate would build the
// mini-evaluator that stance exists to forbid. The supported way to get a
// promoted constant here is `metacall { 2 + 3 }`, which splices a LITERAL —
// and a literal IS promoted, which is what the admit twin pins.
package bc_return_borrow_of_operator_temp_fail;

fn f() -> &i64 { return &(2i64 + 3i64); }

fn main() -> i32 { let p: &i64 = f(); return *p as i32; }
